From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sorabbyngo OS <security@sorabbyngo-os>
Date: Mon, 02 Jun 2026 00:00:00 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 0017] security: heap hardening — poison fill + allocation canary (OpenBSD kern_malloc)

Inspired by OpenBSD sys/kern/kern_malloc.c poison_mem() / poison_check() /
poison_value() machinery and KVA_GUARDPAGES, confirmed present at lines
279, 317-318, 324, 456, 471-472, 568-569 in the checked-out source.

Problem: Patch 0004 (slab guard pages) protects at slab *boundary* level
via guard pages flanking each slab. However:
  - Mid-allocation heap overflows within a slab are not caught.
  - Use-after-free on kmalloc'd objects outside the slab allocator
    (general-purpose heap) produces no signal.
  - The Sorabbyngo LSM (patch 0012) and CML layer process untrusted
    AI API responses into heap buffers — a single UAF or overflow here
    can subvert the NodeID verification path.

Solution: Add a two-layer heap hardening subsystem in
mm/sorabbyngo_malloc_harden.c, adapting OpenBSD's approach:

  Layer 1 — Poison fill (OpenBSD poison_mem / poison_check):
    On kfree(), fill the freed region with a deterministic pattern
    derived from the object's virtual address (poison_value(ptr)).
    On the next allocation of the same slot, verify the pattern is
    intact before handing it to the caller.  A corrupted pattern means
    a write occurred to freed memory (UAF write) — we BUG_ON() with a
    full forensic dump including NodeID and audit chain entry.

  Layer 2 — Allocation canary suffix (OpenBSD chunk_magic / kf_spare0):
    Append a per-allocation 8-byte canary immediately after each
    kmalloc'd object (within the rounded-up allocation size).  The
    canary value is: SipHash(ptr, nodeid_raw, boot_key).  On kfree(),
    verify the canary before zeroing.  A corrupted canary means a
    heap overflow wrote past the object end.

  Scope: applied to SORABBYNGO_SLAB allocations (ksize ≤ 4096) via
  a kmalloc post-alloc / pre-free hook using the existing
  kasan_mempool hooks infrastructure (no KASAN required).

  UAF detection flow:
    kfree(ptr) → poison_fill(ptr, size) → store canary at ptr+size-8
    kmalloc()  → check_poison(ptr, size) → verify canary → return to caller
    UAF write? → poison pattern corrupted → BUG + audit emit

  OpenBSD mapping:
    poison_value(ptr)      → sorabbyngo_poison_value(ptr)
    poison_mem(ptr, sz)    → sorabbyngo_poison_fill(ptr, sz)
    poison_check(ptr, sz)  → sorabbyngo_poison_check(ptr, sz)
    kf_spare0 canary       → *((u64*)(ptr + size - 8)) = canary

  Linux adaptation notes:
    - OpenBSD poison_value() uses (uintptr_t)addr ^ 0xdeadbeef.
      We use SipHash(ptr, nodeid, boot_key) for a non-static pattern
      that's harder to forge if an attacker knows the address.
    - OpenBSD checks at malloc/free time inside the allocator.
      We use mm_slab_free / mm_slab_alloc notifier hooks to stay
      outside the critical allocator path.
    - KVA_GUARDPAGES (line 568) maps to our existing patch 0004.
    - M_ZERO (line 354) already handled by __GFP_ZERO; we skip it.

Signed-off-by: Sorabbyngo OS <security@sorabbyngo-os>
